{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-81576" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the value of $f(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(7x-\\frac{\\pi }{12})+5$ at its local minimum points?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/state.json" "file_sha256" "7568a09f71ba125744ddd3d63a6c71b96bc25d31a87157d98becd8ada3945007"} {"question" "q-81576" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "On a positive branch of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nThe points where $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=1$ are local minimums of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.$ \n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(7x-\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nAdding $5$ to the above inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(7x-\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\ge 1 \\\\  & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(7x-\\frac{\\pi }{12})+5\\ge 1+5 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=6$ at the local minimum points." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/state.json" "file_sha256" "7568a09f71ba125744ddd3d63a6c71b96bc25d31a87157d98becd8ada3945007"} {"question" "q-81576" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bf8664ab3a2236b6d1b537ef70897cd2c92145da573db19f8fd6d22ae198c3ba"} {"question" "q-81576" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/state.json" "file_sha256" "7568a09f71ba125744ddd3d63a6c71b96bc25d31a87157d98becd8ada3945007"} {"question" "q-81576" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/state.json" "file_sha256" "7568a09f71ba125744ddd3d63a6c71b96bc25d31a87157d98becd8ada3945007"} {"question" "q-81576" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/state.json" "file_sha256" "7568a09f71ba125744ddd3d63a6c71b96bc25d31a87157d98becd8ada3945007"} {"question" "q-103912" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Write the equation of the hyperbola $2{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}=18$ in standard form." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/state.json" "file_sha256" "7568a09f71ba125744ddd3d63a6c71b96bc25d31a87157d98becd8ada3945007"} {"question" "q-103912" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Cartesian equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, to write the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to divide both sides of the equation by $18$ and then simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2} & =18 \\\\ \\frac{2{x}^{2}}{18}-\\frac{9{y}^{2}}{18} & =\\frac{18}{18} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/state.json" "file_sha256" "7568a09f71ba125744ddd3d63a6c71b96bc25d31a87157d98becd8ada3945007"} {"question" "q-103912" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bf8664ab3a2236b6d1b537ef70897cd2c92145da573db19f8fd6d22ae198c3ba"} {"question" "q-103912" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/state.json" "file_sha256" "7568a09f71ba125744ddd3d63a6c71b96bc25d31a87157d98becd8ada3945007"} {"question" "q-103912" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/state.json" "file_sha256" "7568a09f71ba125744ddd3d63a6c71b96bc25d31a87157d98becd8ada3945007"} {"question" "q-103912" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/state.json" "file_sha256" "7568a09f71ba125744ddd3d63a6c71b96bc25d31a87157d98becd8ada3945007"} {"question" "q-120347" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Express $2{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})-2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})$ as a single trigonometric ratio." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/state.json" "file_sha256" "7568a09f71ba125744ddd3d63a6c71b96bc25d31a87157d98becd8ada3945007"} {"question" "q-120347" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Our expression can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\n2({cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})-{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}))\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})-{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})-{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/state.json" "file_sha256" "7568a09f71ba125744ddd3d63a6c71b96bc25d31a87157d98becd8ada3945007"} {"question" "q-120347" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bf8664ab3a2236b6d1b537ef70897cd2c92145da573db19f8fd6d22ae198c3ba"} {"question" "q-120347" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/state.json" "file_sha256" "7568a09f71ba125744ddd3d63a6c71b96bc25d31a87157d98becd8ada3945007"} {"question" "q-120347" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})"] ["math" "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})"] ["math" "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})"] ["math" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/state.json" "file_sha256" "7568a09f71ba125744ddd3d63a6c71b96bc25d31a87157d98becd8ada3945007"} {"question" "q-120347" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/state.json" "file_sha256" "7568a09f71ba125744ddd3d63a6c71b96bc25d31a87157d98becd8ada3945007"} {"question" "q-74771" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider a linear transformation $\\,T\\,.$ If $\\,T({v}_{1}+5{v}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,T({v}_{1})=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$, then find $\\,T({v}_{2}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/state.json" "file_sha256" "7568a09f71ba125744ddd3d63a6c71b96bc25d31a87157d98becd8ada3945007"} {"question" "q-74771" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(v+kw)=T(v)+T(kw)=T(v)+kT(w)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor any vectors $v$ and $w.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({v}_{1}+5{v}_{2}) & =T({v}_{1})+5T({v}_{2}) \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]+5T({v}_{2}) \\\\ 5T({v}_{2}) & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ 5T({v}_{2}) & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{2}) & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{2}) & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/state.json" "file_sha256" "7568a09f71ba125744ddd3d63a6c71b96bc25d31a87157d98becd8ada3945007"} {"question" "q-74771" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bf8664ab3a2236b6d1b537ef70897cd2c92145da573db19f8fd6d22ae198c3ba"} {"question" "q-74771" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/state.json" "file_sha256" "7568a09f71ba125744ddd3d63a6c71b96bc25d31a87157d98becd8ada3945007"} {"question" "q-74771" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/state.json" "file_sha256" "7568a09f71ba125744ddd3d63a6c71b96bc25d31a87157d98becd8ada3945007"} {"question" "q-74771" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/state.json" "file_sha256" "7568a09f71ba125744ddd3d63a6c71b96bc25d31a87157d98becd8ada3945007"} {"question" "q-73084" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=-1$ and $f(-1)=-2,$ then what is \n${lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}\\,\\frac{1}{3f(x)-10x}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/state.json" "file_sha256" "7568a09f71ba125744ddd3d63a6c71b96bc25d31a87157d98becd8ada3945007"} {"question" "q-73084" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=-1,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)=f(-1)=-2.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}\\,\\frac{1}{3f(x)-10x} & =\\frac{1}{3{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)-10{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3(-2)-10(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/state.json" "file_sha256" "7568a09f71ba125744ddd3d63a6c71b96bc25d31a87157d98becd8ada3945007"} {"question" "q-73084" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bf8664ab3a2236b6d1b537ef70897cd2c92145da573db19f8fd6d22ae198c3ba"} {"question" "q-73084" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/state.json" "file_sha256" "7568a09f71ba125744ddd3d63a6c71b96bc25d31a87157d98becd8ada3945007"} {"question" "q-73084" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{8}"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/state.json" "file_sha256" "7568a09f71ba125744ddd3d63a6c71b96bc25d31a87157d98becd8ada3945007"} {"question" "q-73084" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/state.json" "file_sha256" "7568a09f71ba125744ddd3d63a6c71b96bc25d31a87157d98becd8ada3945007"} {"question" "q-6731" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\n calculate $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/state.json" "file_sha256" "7568a09f71ba125744ddd3d63a6c71b96bc25d31a87157d98becd8ada3945007"} {"question" "q-6731" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }=-\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/state.json" "file_sha256" "7568a09f71ba125744ddd3d63a6c71b96bc25d31a87157d98becd8ada3945007"} {"question" "q-6731" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bf8664ab3a2236b6d1b537ef70897cd2c92145da573db19f8fd6d22ae198c3ba"} {"question" "q-6731" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/state.json" "file_sha256" "7568a09f71ba125744ddd3d63a6c71b96bc25d31a87157d98becd8ada3945007"} {"question" "q-6731" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/state.json" "file_sha256" "7568a09f71ba125744ddd3d63a6c71b96bc25d31a87157d98becd8ada3945007"} {"question" "q-6731" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/state.json" "file_sha256" "7568a09f71ba125744ddd3d63a6c71b96bc25d31a87157d98becd8ada3945007"} {"question" "q-98719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the equation $\\frac{1}{S+1}+\\frac{2}{S-1}=\\frac{1}{{S}^{2}-1}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/state.json" "file_sha256" "7568a09f71ba125744ddd3d63a6c71b96bc25d31a87157d98becd8ada3945007"} {"question" "q-98719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{S+1}+\\frac{2}{S-1}=\\frac{1}{(S-1)(S+1)}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(S-1,\\,S+1,\\,(S-1)(S+1))=(S-1)(S+1).\n$$\n\n- The values $S=1$ and $S=-1$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{S+1}+\\frac{2}{S-1} & =\\frac{1}{(S-1)(S+1)} \\\\ (S-1)(S+1)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{S+1}+\\frac{2}{S-1}) & =(S-1)(S+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{(S-1)(S+1)} \\\\ \\frac{(S-1)(S+1)}{S+1}+\\frac{2(S-1)(S+1)}{S-1} & =\\frac{(S-1)(S+1)}{(S-1)(S+1)} \\\\ \\frac{(S-1)\\cdot \\cancel{(S+1)}}{\\cancel{S+1}}+\\frac{2(S+1)\\cdot \\cancel{(S-1)}}{\\cancel{S-1}} & =\\frac{\\cancel{(S-1)(S+1)}}{\\cancel{(S-1)(S+1)}} \\\\ S-1+2(S+1) & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $S,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S-1+2(S+1) & =1 \\\\ S-1+2S+2 & =1 \\\\ 3S & =0 \\\\ S & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0\\ne 1$ and $0\\ne -1,$ we conclude that $S=0$ is a valid solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $S=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/state.json" "file_sha256" "7568a09f71ba125744ddd3d63a6c71b96bc25d31a87157d98becd8ada3945007"} {"question" "q-98719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bf8664ab3a2236b6d1b537ef70897cd2c92145da573db19f8fd6d22ae198c3ba"} {"question" "q-98719" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/state.json" "file_sha256" "7568a09f71ba125744ddd3d63a6c71b96bc25d31a87157d98becd8ada3945007"} {"question" "q-98719" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "S=-2"] ["math" "S=-4"] ["math" "S=0"] ["math" "S=3"] ["text" "No solutions"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/state.json" "file_sha256" "7568a09f71ba125744ddd3d63a6c71b96bc25d31a87157d98becd8ada3945007"} {"question" "q-98719" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "S=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/state.json" "file_sha256" "7568a09f71ba125744ddd3d63a6c71b96bc25d31a87157d98becd8ada3945007"} {"question" "q-122298" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "In box $A,$ there are $7$ red marbles and $3$ green marbles. In box $B,$ there are $2$ red marbles and $8$ green marbles. If we randomly select one of the boxes and then we randomly select one marble from that box, what is the probability that it’s a green marble?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/state.json" "file_sha256" "7568a09f71ba125744ddd3d63a6c71b96bc25d31a87157d98becd8ada3945007"} {"question" "q-122298" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $G$ be the event that a green marble is selected, and let $A$ be the event that box $A$ is selected. Then ${A}^{′}$ is the event that box $B$ is selected. \n\nTo calculate the probability that a selected marble is green, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(G)=P(G|A)P(A)+P(G|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since there are $2$ boxes, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=\\frac{1}{2},\\,P({A}^{′})=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n- Since there are $3$ green marbles of the total of $10$ marbles in box $A$ and $8$  green marbles of the total of $10$ marbles in box $B,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(G|A)=\\frac{3}{10},\\,P(G|{A}^{′})=\\frac{8}{10}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a selected marble is green is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(G) & =P(G|A)P(A)+P(G|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{10})(\\frac{1}{2})+(\\frac{8}{10})(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{20}+\\frac{8}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/state.json" "file_sha256" "7568a09f71ba125744ddd3d63a6c71b96bc25d31a87157d98becd8ada3945007"} {"question" "q-122298" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bf8664ab3a2236b6d1b537ef70897cd2c92145da573db19f8fd6d22ae198c3ba"} {"question" "q-122298" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/state.json" "file_sha256" "7568a09f71ba125744ddd3d63a6c71b96bc25d31a87157d98becd8ada3945007"} {"question" "q-122298" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{11}{20}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/state.json" "file_sha256" "7568a09f71ba125744ddd3d63a6c71b96bc25d31a87157d98becd8ada3945007"} {"question" "q-122298" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{11}{20}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/state.json" "file_sha256" "7568a09f71ba125744ddd3d63a6c71b96bc25d31a87157d98becd8ada3945007"} {"question" "q-87327" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Factor $25{a}^{8}+1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/state.json" "file_sha256" "7568a09f71ba125744ddd3d63a6c71b96bc25d31a87157d98becd8ada3945007"} {"question" "q-87327" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the given expression is a sum of squares, since\n\n$$\n25{a}^{8}+1={(5{a}^{4})}^{2}+{1}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTo factor a sum of squares, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${\\text{i}}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25{a}^{8}+1 & =25{a}^{8}-(-1) \\\\  & =25{a}^{8}-({\\text{i}}^{2}) \\\\  & ={(5{a}^{4})}^{2}-{(\\text{i})}^{2} \\\\  & =(5{a}^{4}+\\text{i})(5{a}^{4}-\\text{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/state.json" "file_sha256" "7568a09f71ba125744ddd3d63a6c71b96bc25d31a87157d98becd8ada3945007"} {"question" "q-87327" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bf8664ab3a2236b6d1b537ef70897cd2c92145da573db19f8fd6d22ae198c3ba"} {"question" "q-87327" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/state.json" "file_sha256" "7568a09f71ba125744ddd3d63a6c71b96bc25d31a87157d98becd8ada3945007"} {"question" "q-87327" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(5{a}^{4}+1)(5{a}^{4}-1)"] ["math" "(5{a}^{4}+\\text{i})(5{a}^{4}-\\text{i})"] ["math" "25({a}^{4}+\\text{i})({a}^{4}-\\text{i})"] ["math" "{(5{a}^{2}-\\text{i})}^{2}"] ["math" "{(5{a}^{4}+\\text{i})}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/state.json" "file_sha256" "7568a09f71ba125744ddd3d63a6c71b96bc25d31a87157d98becd8ada3945007"} {"question" "q-87327" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(5{a}^{4}+\\text{i})(5{a}^{4}-\\text{i})"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/state.json" "file_sha256" "7568a09f71ba125744ddd3d63a6c71b96bc25d31a87157d98becd8ada3945007"} {"question" "q-28341" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $16x+4y=12?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/state.json" "file_sha256" "7568a09f71ba125744ddd3d63a6c71b96bc25d31a87157d98becd8ada3945007"} {"question" "q-28341" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of the given line $16x+4y=12,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16x+4y & =12 \\\\ 4y & =-16x+12 \\\\ y & =-4x+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope of the given line is $-4.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y=\\frac{x}{4}-5$ has a slope of $\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n(-4)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=\\frac{x}{4}-5$ is perpendicular to the given line." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/state.json" "file_sha256" "7568a09f71ba125744ddd3d63a6c71b96bc25d31a87157d98becd8ada3945007"} {"question" "q-28341" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bf8664ab3a2236b6d1b537ef70897cd2c92145da573db19f8fd6d22ae198c3ba"} {"question" "q-28341" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/state.json" "file_sha256" "7568a09f71ba125744ddd3d63a6c71b96bc25d31a87157d98becd8ada3945007"} {"question" "q-28341" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-4x+3"] ["math" "y=-\\frac{2}{3}x+2"] ["math" "y=4x+3"] ["math" "y=\\frac{x}{2}-\\frac{5}{2}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{x}{4}-5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/state.json" "file_sha256" "7568a09f71ba125744ddd3d63a6c71b96bc25d31a87157d98becd8ada3945007"} {"question" "q-28341" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=\\frac{x}{4}-5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/state.json" "file_sha256" "7568a09f71ba125744ddd3d63a6c71b96bc25d31a87157d98becd8ada3945007"} {"question" "q-2281" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$\\int ({9}^{x}+3{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/state.json" "file_sha256" "7568a09f71ba125744ddd3d63a6c71b96bc25d31a87157d98becd8ada3945007"} {"question" "q-2281" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Here, we combine the sum rule and our exponential rules to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int ({9}^{x}+3{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {9}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+3\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{9}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}+3{e}^{x}+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/state.json" "file_sha256" "7568a09f71ba125744ddd3d63a6c71b96bc25d31a87157d98becd8ada3945007"} {"question" "q-2281" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013352/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bf8664ab3a2236b6d1b537ef70897cd2c92145da573db19f8fd6d22ae198c3ba"} {"question" "q-2281" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course